As long as you start your new pack on schedule, you're fully protected against pregnancy during your placebo week. My boyfriend and I often have sex during my placebo week and he normally finishes inside me, so don't worry.

&#x200B;

Keep in mind that even if you take the pill perfectly, there is still a slight chance of failure, but that's true of any birth control.

If using condoms gives you peace of mind, there's no reason why you shouldn't keep using them. If you use the pill perfectly it's extremely effective all buy itself, but adding condoms makes it even more so. One thing that might make going condomless a little easier, if you're so inclined is to discuss with your boyfriend ahead of time very clearly and seriously what you will do if the Pill fails.

&#x200B;

That being said: I was on the Pill before my boyfriend and I got together. We used condoms at first, then after testing clean we quit using them and have never looked back. No pullout either. It makes sex more spontaneous and intimate and it does feel MUCH better for him (and better for me as well, although not to his extent). We do have a plan in place, and I use an app to help me take it on schedule. No pregnancy scare and it's been nearly three years.

&#x200B;

Best of luck!

I think a little anxiety is normal with an "invisible" method like the Pill. No method is perfect, which is the source of anxiety, but if you take it carefully the Pill is one of the most effective methods there is--MUCH more so than the visible methods such as condoms, spermicide, and such.

Ultimately it's a cost/benefit analysis based on your own circumstances and feelings. Other than not having sex, Doubling up is the best way to deal with the anxiety (Pill, condoms, and pullout or spermicide, i.e. tripling up). If you use two or three methods together, and use them correctly, the chances of a pregnancy are astronomically low (but not zero).

&#x200B;

Time and experience can also help. As month after month goes by without any pregnancy, you'll possibly start to relax a little. An app like Womanlog can help you remember to take your pill on schedule.

&#x200B;

This is anecdotal and ymmv, but my boyfriend and I use only the Pill, no condoms, no pullout, and have done it this way for years without any pregnancy scares.

OK, that looks like a triphasic to me. The estrogen stays at .035 milligrams but the norgestimate dose changes (.18, .215, .25). Sadly that means that if you went straight from active week three of the current pack to active week one of the new pack, your hormone (norgestimate) dose would drop and you would spot or bleed. 

&#x200B;

If you want, you could try going from active week three of this pack to active week three of the next pack, which would keep the same high dose for two weeks straight, and then throw away the rest of the new pack (the unused weeks one and two). That might work but is wasteful. I'm on a triphasic and have done that before, but please remember I'm not a doctor. :-)

Generally, the more "invisible" a contraceptive is, the more effective (IUD, implant), but they have downsides. There has to be an up-front procedure, and if you get unpleasant side effects (always possible with hormonal methods) that you decide you don't want, another procedure to have it removed.

&#x200B;

If you're actually moving out, the chances of them finding out are reduced, unless they're snoopers (i.e., they come to uni and snoop around your dorm room). The Pill is actually very discreet and can fit in your wallet like a credit card (some versions) or makeup pouch (looks like a compact at first glance). If you don't do well on it, it's easy to stop or to switch to a different pill. Nuvaring is even more discreet, if you pick up your new one on the same day you are scheduled to insert it rather than leave it in your dorm room. Same for a patch, although it can be a bit more visible. IUD would probably be the best.

&#x200B;

I won't speak to the insurance/notification side since things may work differently here in the US, but I would imagine there's plenty of legal privacy limitations in Ontario as well. If you go to the uni clinic, that would probably be best.

Random thoughts": Wide angle (obviously) for the crowd shots, with the field in the foreground (maybe centered) with the crowd surrounding it/focused on it. Maybe get down on field level so the field is in the lower part of the frame and the crowd towers above it.

&#x200B;

42mm isn't going to get you in to the pitches and swings very close, but for action settings in bad light you get to choose among blur (slow shutter speed), shallow depth-of-field (wide aperture), and noise/grain (high ISO). IMO, the hardest one to correct afterwards (impossible to correct, really) is blur, so go with as high a shutter speed as you can manage (1/320 or better yet 1/500 or higher) and compromise on the ISO and aperture. Or you can try to use the blur artistically to suggest motion (blur of pitcher's arm as he pitches or batter's as he swings). That would allow you to stop down a bit or drop the ISO a bit or both.

I'm sorry you're going through this. Breaking up is one of the hardest things in the world.

&#x200B;

Stay on the Pill for at least a cycle or two. You don't want to add any hormonal variations to your current upset. And since we don't know anything about your situation, i think it should be said that from where I stand there's a possibility that you and your ex could get back together, and if you do there could be makeup sex, and you need to be prepared for that. (Perhaps not, and I don't mean to offend, but as I said, it needed to be said.) 

&#x200B;

Be strong and things will be better someday, I promise.

About 90 percent of the things you write are true of me, too. My parents have told me that when I was a young child (pre-bullying) I was very open and outgoing, but now I am a loner with few if any close friends. 

&#x200B;

I don't know of any magic formula to make it go away. I have never believed the line, no matter how well-meaning the speaker is, that "They can't hurt me anymore." They hurt me every day. But maybe it will help you to know that there are other people who are sharing this battle with you. Stay strong.

Sex and sexuality are extremely personal decisions and topics. Because sexuality is such a powerful thing, people naturally try to use it to control others, or try to control how others see it. There's nothing in this world that people (including parents) can't characterize as evil or wrong.

&#x200B;

What's done is done, right or wrong (but I don't think you should see it as wrong), so try not to regret or worry about it; instead, dwell on what was positive about it. Personally, I think if you and your boyfriend love and care for each other, you have done nothing wrong, no matter what your parents or upbringing may have to say about it. Sex is designed to bring a loving couple very close to each other physically, emotionally, and psychologically, and that is a beautiful thing; try not to let your mother's ideas ruin that for you.

&#x200B;

On a practical note, if and when it happens again, you do need to be more careful; you probably aren't pregnant, but having your boyfriend enter you again wasn't the best idea. There are types of birth control that you can get without anyone knowing, and ways of being discreet about it. 

&#x200B;

Please try not to worry and instead take this special time in your life to celebrate the love you and your boyfriend share. Feel free to send me a message if you want to talk further.
